regexec.c: the regset search tests the first byte before it tries a regex

In ONIG_REGSET_POSITION_LEAD mode, for short subjects (up to 500 bytes,
and longer ones too unless every regex of the set has a bounded search
range, the "low/high" case), the search walks the subject one character at a
time and, at each position, asks each regex whether it could match there:
for most regexes, with a forward_search from that position to find the
next candidate. For a regex whose optimizer found what it must start with
at distance 0, with no anchor, a first-byte map (OPTIMIZE_MAP) or an exact
string (OPTIMIZE_STR, _STR_FAST, _STR_FAST_STEP_FORWARD), that forward
search can only report the position itself or a later one, and it reports
the position itself exactly when the map has the byte there, or the string
starts there. With this patch, the search tests that directly at each
position: map[*s], or the first byte and a memcmp of the string. It no
longer runs forward_search for these regexes, neither when it starts nor
at each candidate. match_at still decides every match, the regexes are
still tried in the same order at the same positions, and the long-subject
path is unchanged.

See rust/vendor/README.md.

